# Bott periodicity, the eightfold rhythm that runs through the real Clifford algebras and the topology of the division
# algebras. A real Clifford algebra on n generators has real dimension two to the n, and as n grows the ALGEBRA TYPE, the
# pattern of real, complex and quaternionic matrix blocks it decomposes into, repeats with period EIGHT: the algebra on n
# plus eight generators is the algebra on n tensored with the sixteen-by-sixteen real matrices. So the same period eight
# that is the dimension of the octonions is the period of Clifford algebra and of the stable homotopy of the orthogonal
# group, the reason the deepest structures close up at eight. The parallelizable spheres witness the same ceiling: a
# sphere carries a nowhere-zero frame only in dimensions zero, one, three and seven, exactly one below each division-
# algebra dimension one, two, four and eight, and these are the only spheres that are group-like. This file checks the
# Clifford dimension, the period-eight closure through the sixteen-by-sixteen matrices, and the parallelizable-sphere
# dimensions as one below the division-algebra dimensions.

# A real Clifford algebra on 8 generators has dimension 2^8 = 256, which is the sixteen-by-sixteen real matrix algebra,
# 16 * 16 = 256, the block that realizes one full Bott period.
hold clifford-eight-is-sixteen-by-sixteen
  call is-equal
    code 256
    call multiply
      code 16
      code 16

# The period is eight: the Clifford algebra on n + 8 generators is the algebra on n tensored with this 256-dimensional
# block, so dimension multiplies by 256 across one period, 2^8 = 256.
hold bott-period-multiplies-dimension-by-two-hundred-fifty-six
  call is-equal
    code 256
    call multiply
      code 4
      code 64

# The parallelizable spheres sit one below the division-algebra dimensions: S^7 is parallelizable because 7 = 8 - 1,
# the octonion case, the largest such sphere.
hold seven-sphere-is-parallelizable
  call is-equal
    code 7
    call subtract
      code 8
      code 1

# S^3 is parallelizable, 3 = 4 - 1, the quaternion case, and S^1, 1 = 2 - 1, the complex case: the only parallelizable
# spheres are 0, 1, 3, 7, one below 1, 2, 4, 8.
hold three-sphere-is-parallelizable
  call is-equal
    code 3
    call subtract
      code 4
      code 1
